<h2>The allegations and the arrest</h2>
<p>The FIR of 5 October 2025 described a night-time theft of telecom equipment from an Airtel tower in Narela, in the course of which the complainant\'s colleague was shot. The investigation moved outward from the spot: two co-accused arrested within the month named the applicant, who surrendered in November 2025. Two further pieces of material then entered the record — a judicial test identification parade in which the injured identified the applicant, and the alleged recovery of an improvised pistol with live cartridges at his instance. A chargesheet followed on 5 January 2026, a supplementary chargesheet thereafter, and charges were framed on 8 October 2026.</p>

<h2>Four factors, one direction</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Ballistics</strong><p>The FSL opined that the evidence bullet did not match the test-fired bullets from the recovered pistol — loosening the forensic link between the applicant and the firing.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Parity</strong><p>Every co-accused stood released on bail, and the applicant\'s alleged role was not materially distinguishable.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Stage of the case</strong><p>Investigation was complete and charges framed, so custodial interrogation served no further purpose and tampering risks could be met by conditions.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Article 21</strong><p>About eight months of judicial custody with the trial yet to begin engaged the right to a speedy trial.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Bail law under the BNSS, briefly</h2>
<p>The application was made under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, the successor to Section 439 CrPC, which preserves the High Court\'s concurrent jurisdiction to grant regular bail in non-bailable offences. The substantive considerations are unchanged by the new code: the nature and gravity of the accusation, the character of the supporting evidence, the stage of the investigation and trial, the risk of flight, and the possibility of witnesses being influenced. What this order illustrates is how those abstractions operate in combination — no single factor was decisive, but a weakened forensic case, released co-accused, a completed investigation and mounting custody together crossed the threshold.</p>

<h2>Why identification and recovery did not tip the balance</h2>
<p>The State relied on the test identification parade and the weapon recovery, and both remain live issues for trial. At the bail stage, however, the court weighs rather than adjudicates. The identification came from a single injured witness in a case where the applicant was unnamed in the FIR; the recovery\'s evidentiary weight was qualified by the FSL\'s negative opinion. A bail court asks whether the material, taken at its reasonable highest, justifies continued detention pending trial — and here the Court concluded it did not, while expressly leaving the trial court free to take its own view of the evidence at the appropriate stage.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for bail practice in Delhi</h2>
<p>Three practical points emerge. First, obtain and read the FSL report before arguing a bail application in any firearms case: a mismatch between weapon and projectile is among the most effective bail arguments available, because it is scientific, documentary and undisputed at that stage. Second, parity arguments should be built on a table of co-accused, their alleged roles and their bail orders — courts respond to demonstrated comparability, not assertion. Third, the framing of charges is a hinge moment: once the investigation is over and the trial court is seized of the case, the State\'s argument for custody shifts from investigative need to trial integrity, and well-drafted conditions can usually answer it.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The order was passed in BAIL APPLN. 2079/2026 with CRL.M.A. 17186/2026 and disposes of the application; it binds no finding on guilt or innocence. Offence provisions under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita correspond broadly to the former IPC provisions on attempt to commit culpable homicide, voluntarily causing hurt by dangerous weapons, theft and common intention, alongside Sections 25 and 27 of the Arms Act, 1959.</p>
</div>
